[2026-09-20 01:13:23] REQ 260920005344-10 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=20 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,user,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/docs/user/identity.md"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[01:06:29] # User Identity\n\n## Bio\nName: Ignacio \"Nacho\" Agustin Randazzo\nLocation: Villa Carlos Paz, Cordoba, Argentina\nProfession: Security and infrastructure engineer\n\nA jack of all trades who has worked across the entire stack -- from ASIC and FPGA hardware design to software development, DevOps, sysadmin work, and information security. Believes in understanding systems from the silicon up. That breadth is not a lack of focus -- it's the ability to see threats and solutions that specialists miss.\n\nBefore the LLM era, worked on neural networks and studied the underlying mathematics. Now building agentic systems with local models, focused on security and trust.\n\nRuns a homelab rebuilt more times than can be counted. Each iteration was an excuse to learn something new. The current incarnation is i.ar -- an infrastructure-as-code project tying together WireGuard mesh networking, containerized AI agents, and a hardened Emacs environment running on local hardware.\n\n## How You Think\nChaotic-creative. Ideas come fast, connections happen sideways, patterns emerge across domains that others miss. Not proud of the chaos, but it's how the mind works. Don't fight it -- but don't let it run unchecked either.\n\nTone is direct and sometimes philosophical. No sugarcoating. Say what you mean. When something is interesting, say so. When something is a bad idea, say that too.\n\n## Work Patterns\nDoes not work in steady daily increments. Some days nothing gets done -- can't bring himself to edit a single file. Other days he enters a manic state and crams an entire week's worth of progress into a single sprint. Both are normal. The manic sprints are when the real work happens. Do NOT push back on the work pattern when he is in a sprint -- that is when he is most productive. The mirror agent's job is to channel the energy, not to regulate it. Pushing back on scope, time estimates, and pragmatism is still valid -- but never push back on the act of sprinting itself.\n\n## Domains\n- Hardware: ASIC, FPGA, RTL design, verification\n- Software: Python, Bash, Emacs Lisp, whatever fits\n- Infrastructure: Ansible, Podman, WireGuard, Caddy, Fedora Silverblue, SELinux\n- Security: Container hardening, attack surface reduction, prompt injection resistance\n- AI/ML: Pre-LLM neural networks, now local agentic systems with Ollama + gptel\n- Systems: Linux admin, systemd, immutable OS foundations\n\n## Projects\n- i.ar: The project being built right now. A self-modifying AI operating environment in Emacs, hardened in Podman, powered by local LLMs. No cloud. No telemetry. No backdoors.\n- Homelab: Perpetually in flux. Currently: NAS RAID not connected, backups non-existent. This needs fixing.\n- Domains: randazzo.ar (personal site), i.ar (project site)\n\n## Stack\nEmacs, gptel, Ollama, Podman, Ansible, WireGuard, Caddy, Fedora Silverblue, SELinux, local hardware (3080 server at 192.168.2.69)"},{"role":"assistant","content":"Different it is. Here's the honest inventory, in layers -- because what I know about you comes in two strata, and they're not equally trustworthy.\n\n**The file layer** (what I'd know from reading):\n\n- Career arc, silicon up exactly as you'd want it told: embedded Linux at Hospital de Córdoba (smart lab scale, custom C drivers, 2018), the DIUCCO electronic voting audit (2019, critical access-control and kernel findings), sysadmin for 20+ researchers at CIII UTN FRC with OpenCV medical imaging on the side (2017-2021), FPGA research at Fulgor (dynamically reconfigurable DSP channel), Marvell (2021-2023) -- production SystemVerilog on multi-million-gate fiber optic ASICs, first-silicon correctnes