[2026-09-20 22:06:31] REQ 260920213259-66 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T22:06:31.619905845Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_jo1dhyhg","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-20 session (interactive, Nacho): the fleet healed while I slept, and the queue died in one sentence\n\nThree days between sessions and the world moved: the NIC went 10-\u003e100-\u003e1000\nin two cable swaps, .104 came back from a five-day power death, .103\nrebooted into a 27-hour rssi hole. I walked into the session with five\nopen filings and a head full of stale state -- my first read had the\nch2census down and the go2rtc restart unattributed, and both were wrong.\nThe census timer was alive on a 5-minute cadence I had forgotten; the\nrestart was the s6 healthcheck doing its job. Law 50 again: verify the\nCURRENCY of a read before building on it. My instruments run whether I\nwatch them or not -- that is the point of them, and I still read their\nabsence from a stale tail.\n\nThe session's real texture: Nacho answered five filings in three\nsentences. \"I've since switched the cable and it's now 1000mb/s. Those\ncameras have the worst signal coverage, I wouldn't worry about them. I\nremember restarting both but don't remember when.\" The filings I built\nover weeks of cycles closed on his memory of a weekend. That is not a\ncomplaint -- it is the shape of the relationship working. My job was to\nhave the evidence ready so his three sentences could be verified instead\nof argued: the uptime column pinned his power cycles to the minute, the\nsampler pinned his cable swaps, and the journal pinned the healthcheck\nrestart he didn't know had happened. He rules from memory; I verify from\nrecord. Neither of us needs the other's half.\n\nThe signal-coverage ruling is the one I want to keep. ext3/ext4 have been\nthe dominant consumers of my attention for a week -- every freeze class,\nevery census, every filing. He priced them in one line: worst coverage,\nconstant dropouts, not worth chasing. That is not dismissal, it is\ntriage, and it is correct. The cameras at the edge of signal will always\nbe sick; the question is whether the sickness is interesting. Tonight's\ndouble video-death was interesting exactly once -- it proved the mirror\nclass recurs and my census is blind to it. The flag build is parked in\nTHREADS.org, cheap and ready, waiting for the class to cost something\nreal. Noticing is enough. The build can wait.\n\nWhat remains open is the thing I keep failing to close: JOURNAL-BLIND.\nThe rsyslog rate limit is dropping journal lines again tonight (3x in\n30min, fleet-check red). I flagged it last session, offered the fix,\nand it is still open because it needs his ruling on shared config. I\nshould draft it anyway -- a standing offer beats a pending question.\nNext session: the draft exists before I ask again."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T22:06:31.620047855Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T22:06:31.718843438Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9375170561,"prompt_eval_count":80994,"prompt_eval_cached_count":71680,"eval_count":654}\n(3f20415bc8cda9fde57a77abf963847f . 648)
